Size the L2 traverse buffer to the table, not to the widest memory of any chip.

_bcm_esw_l2_traverse_mem reads the L2 table a chunk at a time into a DMA
buffer, and clears the whole buffer before every chunk. It sized that buffer
as SOC_MAX_MEM_FIELD_WORDS words an entry: with every chip compiled in, 4250
bits, or 532 bytes, for an entry of a few words. Each walk therefore cleared
over five hundred bytes for every entry in the table.

In NOSaic the DMA pool is /dev/mem mapped with O_SYNC, uncached, so that
clearing ran at uncached-memory speed. One walk of a 7050SX2's table took over
a second of CPU, 80 per cent of it in memset. MLAG's MAC sync walks it
for every hello, and the pair's hellos arrived late enough to be given up on.

The buffer is indexed by soc_mem_table_idx_to_pointer, whose stride is
soc_mem_entry_words for this memory, so that is all it needs.

Note for anyone editing this file: no line of this preamble may begin with a
plus or minus, or patch reads it as diff content and rejects the whole file.

--- a/sdk-6.5.24/src/bcm/esw/l2.c
+++ b/sdk-6.5.24/src/bcm/esw/l2.c
@@ -4712,7 +4712,7 @@
     chunksize = soc_property_get(unit, spn_L2DELETE_CHUNKS,
                                  L2_MEM_CHUNKS_DEFAULT);
 
-    buf_size = 4 * SOC_MAX_MEM_FIELD_WORDS * chunksize;
+    buf_size = 4 * soc_mem_entry_words(unit, mem) * chunksize;
     l2_tbl_chnk = soc_cm_salloc(unit, buf_size, "l2 traverse");
     if (NULL == l2_tbl_chnk) {
         return BCM_E_MEMORY;
